<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-murray-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-overall> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Turing RD claim for Holstein coat spotting (tested negative hypothesis) — MorphoEval overall 74.6/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "TESTED NEGATIVE HYPOTHESIS, published as such. Claim tested: the black-and-white spotting of Holstein-Friesian cattle is laid down by a Turing reaction-diffusion pre-pattern with a characteristic wavelength (Murray 1981). Verdict: not supported. A static single-wavelength Turing pattern is rejected on 10 coat-masked specimens (held-out morphometric distance 6.46 vs ~3.3 for body-scale alternatives; the cows carry a few body-scale patches, specimen 01: 3 patches, 99.2% of pigment in one, where the Turing field makes ~125 uniform spots at lambda_c = 10.26 px). A growing-domain RD variant cannot be distinguished from a stochastic melanoblast-colonisation (Eden) control once all masks are measured at the same scale (held-out RD growing-het 3.29 vs Eden 3.43; Eden wins 4/9; paired Wilcoxon p = 0.91; bootstrap 95% CI of the mean difference -0.80..1.14), so adult patch statistics are not evidence for a Turing mechanism in this system. The negative result agrees with the causal genetics: KIT/MITF regulatory variants cause Holstein spotting (Jivanji et al. 2019, 2025) acting through melanoblast survival, proliferation and migration, modelled quantitatively as stochastic colonisation (Mort et al. 2016); no activator-inhibitor morphogen pair is known in cattle skin. Scores below (overall 74.6) rate the simulation package's fidelity and reproducibility, never the truth of the tested claim. Overall 74.6/100 (faithfulness 72.2, reproducibility 80.1); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit 2e5574cb673de7d817650ef02de8f6b9e9e9394f; environment: . Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 74, Morphological realism / specimen match 60, Parameter expressivity 80, Morphometric validation 85, Source and data traceability 90, Code-to-model traceability 74, Reproducibility package 76. Output judged: datasets/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_comparison.png: rendered output of models/1981-murray-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength.py, not present when the audit was snapshotted (unfingerprinted). Supersedes audit: 2026-10-26-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-audit-v6. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Murray (1981) proposed a reaction-diffusion pre-pattern mechanism for mammalian coat markings; Turing (1952) supplied the instability theory. Neither paper measured Holstein coat patterns against the mechanism. | Established cattle genetics: KIT/MITF regulatory variants cause Holstein white spotting (Jivanji et al. 2019 GWAS; Jivanji et al. 2025 causal variants confirmed in edited mice), and mammalian melanoblast colonisation is stochastic migration plus proliferation (Mort et al. 2016).; reconstructed_in_script: Schnakenberg-kinetics RD (not Murray's own kinetics) on static and growing domains with gamma heterogeneity, compared with an Eden-process melanoblast-colonisation control, on 10 coat-masked Holstein specimens at matched measurement scale with paired Wilcoxon/bootstrap statistics (Pass 8). | Published result is negative: static Turing rejected (6.46 vs ~3.3); growing RD vs colonisation indistinguishable (3.29 vs 3.43, p = 0.91). Two blinded independent assessments (54.4, 57.7) overturned two earlier builder conclusions (Passes 4, 7-8) before this verdict was reached.; transfer_boundary: The verdict concerns Holstein-Friesian flank spotting measured by adult patch statistics. It does not transfer to other breeds, other pattern elements (e.g. fine speckling), embryonic pre-pattern stages not observable in adult coat, or other species; Turing mechanisms are experimentally established in other systems (e.g. palate rugae) and nothing here bears on them.; does_not_claim: Does NOT claim the Murray/Turing hypothesis is supported -- the tested claim is reported as a negative result, and the rubric overall (74.6) scores the simulation package, not the hypothesis. | Does NOT claim all reaction-diffusion models are excluded: a growing-domain RD is statistically indistinguishable from colonisation on these statistics, so adult coat morphology alone cannot identify the mechanism either way. | Does NOT claim the Eden control is the true mechanism; it is a minimal colonisation proxy, and its founder density was selected on specimen 01 (disclosed). | Does NOT claim independent confirmation of the Pass 8+ verdict: the Pass 9 assessment is builder-scored (independent re-score is remediation R11), held-out n = 9 specimens limits power, and coat segmentation is a saturation heuristic.." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/muse-wonder-1.0-high> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
    pk:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: {'id': 'R11', 'item': 'Independent re-score of the Pass-8+ tree before any Eden-vs-RD claim is quoted (the published negative verdict on static Turing does not depend on it; the RD-vs-colonisation null does)'}",
        "remediation needed: {'id': 'R9', 'item': 'Cite Eden (1961) / a melanoblast migration source for the control; consider a migration-wave (neural-crest front) variant, whose enclaves invade from edges like real Holstein white'}" ;
    pk:hasScore 7.46e-01 ;
    pk:hasVerdict "mostly faithful" .

